"Agentic In-Context Learning: What Algorithms Can Transformers Learn in Closed-Loop Interaction? (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23051v1)\nciteturn41230view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23051v1\",\"lineno\":185}); Total lines: 1027\nL123: Training objectives and diagnosis (Section cite17†5 ). We theoretically characterize when process supervision preserves outcome-optimal behavior, then develop behavioral diagnostics for trained Transformers.\nL124: \nL125:   * •\nL126: \nL127: What training learns (Section cite20†6 ). Supervised distillation tests learned execution of supplied interactive recursions. RL without oracle action shows how outcome and process rewards lead to different decisions, and how those decisions change with controllable reward intervention.\nL128: ## 2 Related Work\nL129: #### Learning algorithm views of ICL and ICRL.\nL130: ICL has been characterized as implicit Bayesian inference, regression, gradient descent, algorithm selection, and data-dependent learning-to-learn (cite83†Xie et al., 2022 ; cite84†Garg et al., 2022 ; cite85†Akyürek et al., 2023 ; cite86†von Oswald et al., 2023 ; cite87†Bai et al., 2023 ; cite88†Wu et al., 2025 ).\nL131: Constructive, behavioral, and mechanistic studies identify familiar estimators inside Transformers (cite85†Akyürek et al., 2023 ; cite86†von Oswald et al., 2023 ; cite87†Bai et al., 2023 ); we extend this standard to closed-loop recursions whose decisions determine subsequent in-context data.\nL132: ICRL extends this perspective to RL updates and control, including pretraining on stored state-action-reward trajectories with source-policy or oracle actions (cite93†Laskin et al., 2023 ; cite94†Lee et al., 2023 ; cite95†Lin et al., 2024 ). These models can nevertheless generate their own interaction histories at deployment.\nL133: Prior work implements posterior sampling, bandit, TD, SARSA, or policy-improvement computations and distinguishes model-free from model-based strategies (cite96†Wang et al., 2025 ; cite99†Fang and Rajan, 2025 ; cite97†Liang and Lai, 2026 ). Our interface permits both reward-hidden and history-dependent feedback.\nL134: Rewards used for training and evaluation need not be revealed in the agent’s transcript, and individual returned records need not be Markov states: the feedback law may depend on the full interaction history, from which the learner constructs its sufficient state. Agentic ICL unifies active learning, bandits, online RL, and tool use. The primary discovery experiments provide neither oracle actions nor sufficient-state targets.\nL135: #### Tool agents and interactive language feedback.\nL136: Tool agents append search results, test outputs, or other feedback to the context (cite89†Yao et al., 2023 ; cite90†Shinn et al., 2023 ; cite91†Zhao et al., 2024 ; cite92†Lutz et al., 2024 ), but are usually evaluated by success or transfer. Work on language feedback instead studies when language feedback is informative enough to enable efficient online learning (cite100†Xu et al., 2026 ) or how to incorporate teacher-selected critique (cite101†Klissarov et al., 2026 ).\nL137: We treat language as one possible returned record and compare candidate descriptions of the model’s state, update rule, and decision rule, separating compilation from behavior learned with terminal outcome reward. “Agentic in-context learning” has also named a stance-aggregation method (cite102†Lee et al., 2025 ); we use the term for the interaction regime in Definition cite103†3.1 .\nL138: #### Closed-loop learning settings and algorithms.\nL139: We focus on learning settings in which the learner’s input history is generated online through interaction: its actions affect which observations enter context, rather than merely processing a dataset fixed in advance. This includes adaptive data collection, where decisions determine which labels, measurements, rewards, or state transitions arrive next.\nL140: In active learning, previously observed labels guide the choice of subsequent queries to improve downstream predictions (cite104†Settles, 2009 ); bandits reveal selected-arm rewards (cite105†Auer et al., 2002a ; cite106†Li et al., 2010 ; cite107†Russo et al., 2018 ; cite108†Russo et al., 2026 ); and online RL also controls persistent state and long-horizon return (cite109†Sutton and Barto, 2018 ; cite110†Zanette and Brunskill, 2018 ).\nL141: We instantiate BALD for active learning; Thompson sampling, UCB, LinUCB, and EXP3 for bandits; and Q-learning, SARSA, actor-critic, and learned-model planning for online RL, alongside VOI and KG for adaptive query selection (cite111†Frazier et al., 2008 ; cite112†Auer et al., 2002b ; cite113†Watkins and Dayan, 1992 ; cite114†Azar et al., 2017 ). Bayesian updating, optimism, and planning recur across these settings.\nL142: #### Transformer expressivity for recursive algorithms.\nL143: \nL144: Universal approximation and recurrent-depth Transformers establish broad expressivity (cite115†Yun et al., 2020 ; cite116†Dehghani et al., 2019 ). Our construction exposes a sufficient-state wavefront, shares parameters across turns, and converts per-action approximation into an autonomous return bound.\nL145: #### Process supervision and reward shaping.\nL146: \nL147: Process supervision scores intermediate steps, but shaping can change the optimal policy unless action-value ordering is preserved (cite117†Lightman et al., 2023 ; cite118†Cui et al., 2025 ; cite119†Cheng et al., 2025 ; cite120†Ng et al., 1999 ). We use potential-based shaping to construct an outcome-preserving process-reward control.\nL148: ## 3 Agentic ICL: A Closed-Loop Learning Framework\nL149: \nL150: Consider an LLM agent asked to solve a coding task. It searches for documentation, executes code, and uses the returned pages or errors to decide what to try next. Each action changes its future evidence. We separate this process into the interaction that generates the transcript and the learner computation that updates evidence and selects actions.\nL151: ### 3.1 Interaction with the Environment\nL152: We first specify how the agent’s actions determine the observations added to its context. Let $x_{0}$ be the initial prompt, containing the task description and any public information available before interaction. Fix a task $M$, which contains the task parameters and any fixed episode-level latent state, with environment kernel $K_{M}$, a finite action space, and horizon $H$. Write $\\pi_{\\theta}$ for the action policy induced by the frozen causal Transformer $\\mathcal{T}_{\\theta}$.\nL153: Define $h_{0}=x_{0}$ and $h_{t}=(h_{t-1},a_{t},x_{t})$, where $a_{t}$ is the $t$-th action and $x_{t}$ its returned record. Before action $t$, the model receives $h_{t-1}$.\nL154: ###### Definition 3.1 (Agentic in-context learning).\nL155: \nL156: An Agentic ICL episode is the closed loop\nL157: \nL158:  | $$a_{t}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid h_{t-1}),\\qquad x_{t}\\sim K_{M}(\\cdot\\mid h_{t-1},a_{t}),\\qquad t=1,\\ldots,H,$$  |  | (1)\nL159: \nL160: This is called Agentic ICL as the parameters $\\theta$ remain fixed during deployment, and learning in context occurs through the dependence of $\\pi_{\\theta}$ on the growing transcript $h_{t-1}$.\nL161: This definition specifies the execution regime. For an LLM agent, $\\pi_{\\theta}$ is typically a Transformer.\nL162: ### 3.2 Interactive Learning Algorithm\nL163: \nL164: In the coding example, the agent maintains an information state, updates it after each tool result, and uses it to select the next search or code execution. Thus an interactive learner can be described by a sufficient state $s_{t}$ before action $t$, an update $U_{t}$, a decision rule $G_{t}$, and legal actions $\\mathcal{L}_{t}(s_{t})$. Coupled to the environment step in Definition cite103†3.1 , its learner’s recursion is\nL165:  | $\\displaystyle s_{1}\\!=\\!U_{0}(x_{0}),~a_{t}\\!\\sim\\!G_{t}(\\cdot\\mid s_{t}),~s_{t+1}\\!=\\!U_{t}(s_{t},a_{t},x_{t}),~t\\!=\\!1,\\ldots,H.$  |  | (2)\nL166: \nL167: The induced history policy is $G_{t}(\\cdot\\mid s_{t}(h_{t-1}))$. The environment $K_{M}$ supplies feedback, while the learner maps $(U_{t},G_{t})$ incorporate it and select the next action.\nL168: This factorization separates how evidence is incorporated from how actions are selected. Consider three representative and widely studied settings of learning problems: active learning selects queries and receives labels to improve downstream predictions; bandits select an arm and reveal its outcome for cumulative reward or regret; and online RL controls both immediate feedback and future states for long-horizon return or regret.\nL169: They are all included in this framework and distinguished by how actions shape future context. At the interface level, any finite-horizon policy can be expressed in this recursive form by taking $s_{t}=h_{t-1}$. Importantly, the feedback need not itself be the task reward, separating the records used for in-context adaptation from the task objective. Appendix cite29†A gives instantiations.\nL170: ## 4 Compiling Interactive Learning Algorithms into Transformers\nL171: \nL172: The framework expresses an interactive algorithm through its state, update, and decision rule. We now construct a frozen causal Transformer that executes (cite121†2 ) by serializing the transcript, routing the state between turns, incorporating feedback, and producing the next action distribution.\nL173: \nL174: Assume fixed-width encoders $E_{x}$ and $E_{a}$ for public records and actions. We serialize the prompt and extend it recursively, for $1\\leq t<H$:\nL175:  | $\\displaystyle\\operatorname{Prompt}(h_{0})$  | $\\displaystyle=[E_{x}(x_{0}),\\mathtt{SUM},\\mathtt{ACT}],$  |  | (3)\nL176:  | $\\displaystyle\\operatorname{Prompt}(h_{t})$  | $\\displaystyle=[\\operatorname{Prompt}(h_{t-1}),E_{a}(a_{t}),E_{x}(x_{t}),\\mathtt{SUM},\\mathtt{ACT}].$  |\nL177: Each field occupies one vector token. The $\\mathtt{SUM}$ token provides workspace for the learner state; the final $\\mathtt{ACT}$ token emits the next action. The action and its returned record are then appended to form the next prompt. This encoding makes the learner’s computation explicit. It is a non-exclusive abstraction of LLM agents’ data flow, not a lossless encoding of arbitrary natural-language records.\nL178: Given this serialization, the following theorem states that a frozen causal Transformer can uniformly realize the policy of any regular finite-horizon recursion with arbitrarily small error. Appendix cite45†D gives the proof.\nL179: ###### Theorem 4.1 (Interactive-algorithm compilation).\nL180: Fix $H$ and a learner of the form in (cite121†2 ). Assume compact fixed-width encodings of records, actions, and learner states; uniformly Lipschitz initialization, updates, and finite decision logits admitting feed-forward approximation; and a finite action vocabulary with a public-history legality mask. Let each decision kernel be the masked softmax of these logits. For every $\\epsilon>0$, there is a frozen causal Transformer of depth at most $O(H)$ satisfying\nL181:  | $$\\max_{1\\leq t\\leq H}\\sup_{\\text{legal }h_{t-1}}\\operatorname{TV}\\!\\left(\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid\\operatorname{Prompt}(h_{t-1})),G_{t}(\\cdot\\mid s_{t}(h_{t-1}))\\right)\\leq\\epsilon.$$  |\nL182: \nL183: Table cite122†1 illustrates how representative learners instantiate the state-update-decision interface. Both the settings and the algorithms within each setting are illustrative rather than exhaustive. Details are provided in Appendix cite29†A .\nL184: Table 1: Illustrative settings and algorithms covered by Theorem cite123†4.1 .\nL185: Setting  | State $s_{t}$  | Update $U_{t}$  | Decision $G_{t}$ / examples\nL186: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL187: Active learning  | Labeled set, query pool, posterior  | Add queried label, update posterior  | BALD, value-of-information query selection\nL188: Bandits  | Posterior or reward statistics  | Posterior or statistical update  | Thompson sampling, UCB\nL189: Online RL  | Values, policy parameters, or learned model  | TD, policy-gradient, or model update  | Q-learning, SARSA, actor-critic, learned-model planning\nL190: ## 5 Training Objectives and Behavioral Diagnosis\nL191: \nL192: Section cite16†4 establishes that Transformers can represent these interactive policies. We next characterize which decisions different training objectives favor, and introduce diagnostics for examining the policies beyond task performance alone. We first define task value to evaluate their autonomous performance.\nL193: Let $M\\sim\\rho$ be a task drawn from the training or evaluation distribution, and let $R_{M}(h_{H})\\in[0,B]$ score the final trajectory. For any policy $\\pi$, its expected task value is\nL194: \nL195:  | $$V_{M}(\\pi)=\\mathbb{E}_{h_{H}\\sim(\\pi,K_{M})}R_{M}(h_{H}).$$  |  | (4)\nL196: \nL197: The return $R_{M}(h_{H})$ is trainer-side information and need not be revealed in $x_{t}$.\nL198: Theorem cite123†4.1 also controls this task value: if policies agree within $\\epsilon$ at every legal history, autonomous return differs by at most $BH\\epsilon$ (Proposition cite124†F.1 , Appendix cite57†F ).\nL199: ### 5.1 What is the optimal behavior under a specific supervision objective?\nL200: \nL201: We consider outcome reward and process reward, which are commonly used in RL post-training of language model agents. We characterize when maximizing cumulative process reward preserves the actions that maximize the final outcome. For a supervision objective $S$, let $\\mathcal{R}_{S,t}(h_{H})$ be its trainer-side return from decision $t$ onward and $\\mu$ a continuation policy. Define\nL202:  | $$Q_{S,t}^{*}(h,a)=\\sup_{\\mu}\\mathbb{E}^{\\mu}[\\mathcal{R}_{S,t}(H_{H})\\mid H_{t-1}=h,A_{t}=a],\\qquad\\mathcal{A}_{S,t}^{*}(h)=\\arg\\max_{\\text{legal }a}Q_{S,t}^{*}(h,a).$$  |  | (5)\nL203: \nL204: A policy is $S$-optimal at $h$ when its support lies in $\\mathcal{A}_{S,t}^{*}(h)$. This defines objective-optimal behavior, not a unique implementation or a finite-sample convergence guarantee.\nL205: Terminal outcome reward provides the simplest instance. With terminal task return $R_{M}(h_{H})$, set $\\mathcal{R}_{\\mathrm{out},t}(h_{H})=R_{M}(h_{H})$ and write $Q_{\\mathrm{out},t}^{*}(h,a)=\\sup_{\\mu}\\mathbb{E}^{\\mu}[R_{M}(H_{H})\\mid H_{t-1}=h,A_{t}=a],~\\mathcal{A}_{\\mathrm{out},t}^{*}(h)=\\arg\\max_{\\text{legal }a}Q_{\\mathrm{out},t}^{*}(h,a).$ Then $\\mathcal{A}_{\\mathrm{out},t}^{*}(h)$ is the set of terminal-outcome-optimal actions at $h$.\nL206: For process supervision with step rewards $\\widetilde{r}_{t}^{S}$ and discount $\\beta$, set $\\mathcal{R}_{S,t}(h_{H})=\\sum_{u=t}^{H}\\beta^{u-t}\\widetilde{r}_{u}^{S},~Q_{S,t}^{*}(h,a)=\\sup_{\\mu}\\mathbb{E}^{\\mu}[\\mathcal{R}_{S,t}(H_{H})\\mid H_{t-1}=h,A_{t}=a].$ Process-optimal behavior is determined by cumulative action values, not isolated step scores. To provide dense supervision while preserving outcome-optimal behavior, the process reward must satisfy the ordering condition below.\nL207: ###### Proposition 5.1 (When process supervision preserves outcome-optimal behavior).\nL208: \nL209: On any history set, every process-optimal behavior is also outcome-optimal if and only if\nL210: \nL211:  | $$\\mathcal{A}_{S,t}^{*}(h)\\subseteq\\mathcal{A}_{\\mathrm{out},t}^{*}(h)\\quad\\text{at every history }h.$$  |  | (6)\nL212: \nL213: A strict ordering reversal makes process-optimal behavior outcome-suboptimal; a process tie that includes an outcome-suboptimal action permits the two behaviors to differ.\nL214: A common way to densify supervision is to assign local step rewards (cite120†Ng et al., 1999 ; cite117†Lightman et al., 2023 ; cite118†Cui et al., 2025 ) but this can violate the ordering condition above. A particularly myopic score $\\widetilde{r}(x_{t-1},a)$ depends only on the latest record and can overlook earlier evidence that changes the value of an action. Appendix cite42†B proves the proposition.\nL215: And when outcome rewards are available, potential-based shaping can provide intermediate rewards while preserving outcome-optimal action sets (Appendix cite44†C ).\nL216: ### 5.2 Which candidate algorithm best describes the model’s actions?\nL217: \nL218: To examine what training produces, we compare the learned policy with candidate algorithms on histories where their decisions differ. For candidate policies $\\pi^{1},\\ldots,\\pi^{L}$ and legal histories $\\mathcal{D}=\\{(h_{i},w_{i})\\}_{i=1}^{N}$ with $w_{i}\\geq 0$ and $\\sum_{i}w_{i}=1$, define\nL219:  | $$d_{\\mathcal{D}}(\\pi,\\mu)=\\sum\\nolimits_{i=1}^{N}w_{i}\\,\\operatorname{TV}\\!\\left(\\pi(\\cdot\\mid h_{i}),\\mu(\\cdot\\mid h_{i})\\right).$$  |  | (7)\nL220: \nL221: The set is algorithm-separating at scale $\\Delta_{\\mathcal{D}}>0$ when every candidate pair is at least that far apart. Because ordinary rollouts may miss budget, posterior, or confidence boundaries where algorithms disagree, we find legal high-disagreement histories by exact search and freeze $\\mathcal{D}$ before inspecting the Transformer.\nL222: ###### Proposition 5.2 (Identification on separating histories).\nL223: \nL224: Suppose $\\mathcal{D}$ is algorithm-separating at scale $\\Delta_{\\mathcal{D}}$, and for some $k$, $d_{\\mathcal{D}}(\\pi,\\pi^{k})\\leq\\epsilon$. Then $\\pi^{k}$ is the unique closest candidate whenever $\\Delta_{\\mathcal{D}}>2\\epsilon$. If every empirically estimated candidate distance has absolute error at most $\\zeta$, the empirical nearest candidate is still $k$ whenever $\\Delta_{\\mathcal{D}}>2\\epsilon+2\\zeta$.\nL225: This follows directly from the triangle inequality and provides a sufficient condition for stable nearest-candidate identification. This diagnostic identifies the closest policy within the specified candidate family. It does not identify a unique internal algorithm.\nL226: ## 6 Experiments: Executing and Discovering Interactive Algorithms\nL227: Our experiments examine learned execution and objective-dependent decisions. Section cite21†6.1 introduces an exactly diagnosable environment and task; Section cite22†6.2 tests learned compilation from oracle algorithm supervision, to support Theorem cite123†4.1 ; Section cite23†6.3 diagnoses behavior under outcome and process objectives and how it changes under objective interventions, supporting Proposition cite125†5.1 ; Section cite25†6.4 confirms these results using a pretrained LLM with natural-language tools.\nL228: Unless noted otherwise, metrics are averaged within each seed and reported as mean $\\pm$ standard deviation across five independently trained seeds.\nL229: ### 6.1 BTS: an exactly diagnosable Agentic ICL environment\nL230: Multi-round Bayesian Tool Selection (BTS) models sequential tool use while keeping its optimal policy exactly computable. Each task asks the agent to predict a hidden target $Z\\in[K]$ using tools $e\\in[E]$ with likelihoods $L_{e}(y\\mid z)=\\Pr(Y=y\\mid Z=z,\\text{tool }e)$, costs $c_{e}$, discount $\\delta$, and a query budget.\nL231: A tool-call action $e$ returns an observation $Y\\sim L_{e}(\\cdot\\mid Z)$, which is appended to the next prompt; an answer action $g\\in[K]$ terminates the episode with reward $\\delta^{C}\\mathbf{1}\\{g=Z\\}$, where $C$ is the total call cost. Tools are legal only while calls remain and their costs fit the budget; answers are always legal. The initial prompt contains all task-specific parameters, and is followed by the action-evidence transcript.\nL232: Bayes dynamic programming (Bayes DP) computes an optimal policy for this task. At history $h_{t-1}$, Bayes DP makes decisions from a sufficient state, consisting of the posterior $b_{t}(z)=\\Pr(Z=z\\mid h_{t-1})$, remaining call horizon, and remaining budget. It compares immediate answers with the discounted value of future adaptive queries and is exactly outcome-optimal in this finite known environment. Appendix cite58†G gives details of this optimal policy.\nL233: Priors, likelihood matrices, labels, costs, discounts, budgets, and query horizons are randomized across episodes, preventing a fixed tool-calling script from matching the task-specific oracle. We evaluate autonomous return on fresh tasks and exact action probabilities on frozen diagnostic histories. Longer-horizon results control for budget feasibility and realized interaction length; Appendix cite58†G gives the complete task distribution and protocol.\nL234: ### 6.2 Learned compilation: can Transformers execute the recursion?\nL235: The construction establishes the existence of Transformer parameters that execute interactive recursions. We now test whether supervised training can recover such behavior. We select representative algorithms whose states, updates, and policies can all be computed exactly, allowing us to check each part of the learned recursion. We train a Transformer by supervised distillation from three such algorithms: Bayes DP for active learning, Beta-Bernoulli UCB for bandits, and tabular Q-learning for online RL.\nL236: Within the same interface, these algorithms require Bayesian updating with planning, count-sum confidence statistics, and bootstrapped value updates, respectively.\nL237: Figure cite126†2 compares distilled models with task-specific random Transformer baselines on action choices, state reconstruction, updates, and autonomous performance, and shows the change in action TV at longer horizons; lower is better. Appendix cite64†G.4 gives full baselines, training details, and metric definitions.\nL238: Figure 2: Learned execution from supervised distillation. Each panel compares a distilled model with its corresponding random Transformer (null baseline). $x-$ticks are different metrics. Full values are provided in Appendix Tables cite127†12 and cite128†13 .\nL239: Figure cite126†2 shows that the distilled models closely match all three supplied recursions. Distillation substantially reduces every metric relative to the corresponding random Transformer baseline. Thus optimization can reach qualitatively different realizations permitted by Theorem cite123†4.1 . Bayes DP and UCB degrade less than Q-learning on their respective longer-horizon evaluations, consistent with the additive-state distinction in Appendix cite54†E , while the length extrapolation remains empirical.\nL240: ### 6.3 Training objectives change which candidate best matches the model\nL241: Distillation demonstrates learned execution when the target policy is supplied. We next remove oracle action and state targets and train only from rewards, as in RL post-training of language model agents. The central question is how training objectives shape candidate-relative decisions. In particular, we test whether learned behavior tracks changes in the training objective under controlled interventions. We consider four supervision conditions.\nL242: Outcome reward targets terminal BTS return, for which Bayes DP is optimal; one-step process reward scores immediate local progress; outcome-preserving process reward adds a potential-based shaping term with a fixed bounded perturbation; and Bayes DP-score distillation provides a supervised reference using a softmax over unregularized Bayes DP action values.\nL243: A verifier scores immediate information gain or apparent progress without computing globally optimal continuation values. Its three parameters $(\\alpha,\\tau_{v},\\gamma)$ control the local score and credit assignment. A call is rewarded when its realized gain in answerability and distributional certainty offsets its cost.\nL244: The confidence weight $\\alpha$ trades top-answer probability against normalized certainty, $\\tau_{v}$ controls how strongly each observation updates the verifier belief, and the credit discount $\\gamma$ controls how much later verifier progress is assigned to the current action. The primary setting uses $(\\alpha,\\tau_{v},\\gamma)=(.75,.75,0)$, and we refer to its $\\gamma=0$ objective as the one-step process reward.\nL245: Following Proposition cite125†5.1 , the outcome-preserving process reward uses a perturbed potential-shaped dense signal while preserving the outcome-optimal policy set.\nL246: To describe the learned policy with (cite129†7 ), here we consider four candidates sharing the Bayesian update but using different decision rules: Bayes DP, one-step knowledge gradient (KG), one-step entropy reduction, and an open-loop oracle. These candidates distinguish outcome-optimal planning, one-step value of information, a surrogate information objective, and feedback-insensitive acquisition.\nL247: Appendix cite58†G gives the formal definitions of the rewards and candidate policies, the diagnostic histories, and the complete training protocol.\nL248: Figure 3: Distances to candidate policies under four training objectives. Each panel reports policy TV to the four candidates on a shared diagnostic set.\nL249: Figure cite130†3 shows that outcome reward training produces behavior closest to Bayes DP, whereas one-step process reward training produces behavior closest to KG. The latter is consistent with the one-step objective violating the ordering condition in Proposition cite125†5.1 . The outcome-preserving process reward instead remains Bayes DP-like. The bounded perturbation changes individual step rewards, but telescoping leaves the ordering of cumulative action values unchanged.\nL250: This provides an empirical test of the proposition’s ordering criterion without requiring the process and outcome rewards to be numerically equal. Appendix Table cite131†8 reports the full candidate comparison. We also perform a 12-cell sweep over $(\\alpha,\\tau_{v})$ (Appendix cite68†G.6 ). The primary $(.75,.75)$ result is not an isolated parameter setting: the nearest candidate changes with the verifier reward specification, while the primary KG-like behavior persists across a neighborhood of tested settings.\nL251: All tested one-step reward settings with $\\gamma=0$ exhibit ordering violations on the diagnostic set and are closest to candidates other than Bayes DP.\nL252: #### Verifier calibration and credit assignment.\nL253: We next isolate credit assignment $\\gamma$ while holding the local score $(\\alpha,\\tau_{v})$ fixed. We vary $\\gamma$ to test when the objective satisfies the ordering condition in Proposition cite125†5.1 . We fix $\\alpha=\\tau_{v}=1$ because at $\\gamma=0$, its optimal-action set coincides with that of one-step KG; at $\\gamma=1$, its expected cumulative return equals the expected outcome return up to an action-independent baseline, so its optimal-action set coincides with that of Bayes DP.\nL254: Now the ordering condition in Proposition cite125†5.1 depends on $\\gamma$ and on the histories being evaluated. We prespecify a margin-separated set of diagnostic histories that distinguish Bayes DP from KG and use it to evaluate how ordering preservation and learned behavior change with $\\gamma$.\nL255: Figure 4: Varying the credit discount $\\gamma$ at $\\alpha=\\tau_{v}=1$. The gray band marks the common ordering/decision crossover; the green band marks zero oracle violations, after which learned agreement is nearly constant.\nL256: Figure cite132†4 tests Proposition cite125†5.1 ’s ordering criterion empirically. Panel (a) reports the fractions of diagnostic histories satisfying or violating $\\mathcal{A}_{S,t}^{*}(h)\\subseteq\\mathcal{A}_{\\mathrm{out},t}^{*}(h)$, and panel (b) reports the learned policies’ top-action agreement with Bayes DP and KG. Both pairs of curves cross between $\\gamma=.50$ and $.70$.\nL257: On this diagnostic set, the proposition’s strict condition is reached between $.90$ and $.95$, when the violation rate becomes zero; thereafter learned agreement and terminal return remain nearly constant. Thus extending credit moves the objective from KG-like local ordering to Bayes DP-consistent ordering, and the trained policies follow the same transition.\nL258: Appendix cite70†G.8 describes how we construct the diagnostic sets and reports agreement with each objective’s optimal actions, minimum action-value gaps, and comparisons with additional candidates.\nL259: Returning to the state–update–decision decomposition, posterior decoding and update-identity checks yield similar errors across objectives despite their different decisions. These supporting diagnostics and controlled decision tests are reported in Appendix cite61†G.3 .\nL260: ### 6.4 Natural-language tool use with a pretrained LLM\nL261: The symbolic experiments show that learned decisions change with the training objective. We now test whether these differences persist when a pretrained language model receives the same task information through natural-language descriptions. We fine-tune Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (cite133†Qwen et al., 2025 ) on BTS with outcome reward, one-step process reward, and Bayes DP-score distillation, then evaluate training templates, unseen paraphrases, and a shuffled-description control.\nL262: Appendix cite76†G.9 gives the protocol.\nL263: Figure 5: Natural-language tool use under three training objectives. Top: paired bars compare distances to Bayes DP and KG references; the closer candidate is unchanged under unseen paraphrases. Bottom: task returns decrease modestly under paraphrases and sharply under shuffled cards, and move all policies far from both references. Appendix Table cite134†24 reports all values.\nL264: Figure cite135†5 shows that these behavioral differences persist with language inputs and unseen paraphrases. Outcome reward training is Bayes DP-like, whereas one-step process reward training is KG-like. Distillation most closely matches its supplied Bayes DP reference. Appendix Table cite136†25 compares the models with additional candidates. The same nearest-candidate relationships persist across unseen paraphrases with modest return degradation.\nL265: Shuffling tool cards instead collapses returns and moves every policy far from both candidates. Though the model receives numerical observation models and a fixed action vocabulary that departures from real agentic task, generalization across task and tool paraphrases, together with the shuffled-description control, indicates dependence on description semantics rather than a fixed token-level rule.\nL266: ## 7 Conclusion and Limitations\nL267: We formalize Agentic ICL as interactive adaptation in which a fixed-parameter Transformer shapes its future evidence through actions, without requiring observations to reveal rewards. Our construction realizes regular finite-horizon recursions with a bound on autonomous return loss, and distillation demonstrates learned execution.\nL268: Reward experiments link decision differences to outcome-optimal action ordering: a perturbed process reward that preserves this ordering retains behavior close to outcome training, while objective interventions shift learned decisions as alignment changes. The outcome/process decision contrast also persists across textual paraphrases in a pretrained language model.\nL269: The theory establishes expressivity under regularity assumptions without guaranteeing optimization convergence. Behavioral comparisons are candidate-relative, and probes do not identify a unique internal algorithm. Experiments use small structured tasks, hand-specified verifiers, and a potential-based control whose perturbation preserves optimal actions exactly. These results do not establish robustness to arbitrary process-reward errors or learned verifiers.\nL270: Language-model tests supply observation models and constrain actions, leaving generalization to open-ended agents with unknown tool semantics, longer interactions, and context compression unresolved.\nL271: ## 8 AI Use Statement\nL272: In this work, we used generative AI tools to assist with proof writing and method implementation, obtain feedback on research methodology and experiments, propose or refine hypotheses, and generate synthetic datasets. We did not use these tools to develop theoretical models or conceptual frameworks, formulate mathematical claims, provide critical ingredients for mathematical proofs, translate text, conduct qualitative or thematic data analysis, or interpret results.\nL273: Dataset cleaning and reformatting are not applicable to this work. We reviewed all AI-assisted work. We checked LLM-generated proofs for errors and tested generated code for correctness. We take responsibility for all final text, claims, and artifacts, including AI-assisted content.\nL274: ## References\nL275:   * Akyürek et al. (2023) E. Akyürek, D. Schuurmans, J. Andreas, T. Ma, and D. Zhou What learning algorithm is in-context learning? investigations with linear models. In International Conference on Learning Representations, Cited by: cite137†§1 , cite138†§2 .\nL276:   * Auer et al. (2002a) P. Auer, N. Cesa-Bianchi, and P. Fischer Finite-time analysis of the multiarmed bandit problem. Machine Learning. Cited by: cite139†Appendix A , cite140†§2 .\nL277:   * Auer et al. (2002b) P. Auer, N. Cesa-Bianchi, Y. Freund, and R. E. Schapire The nonstochastic multiarmed bandit problem. SIAM Journal on Computing. Cited by: cite141†Appendix A , cite140†§2 .\nL278:   * Azar et al. (2017) M. G. Azar, I. Osband, and R. Munos Minimax regret bounds for reinforcement learning. In International Conference on Machine Learning, Cited by: cite142†Appendix A , cite140†§2 .\nL279:   * Bai et al. (2023) Y. Bai, F. Chen, H. Wang, C. Xiong, and S. Mei Transformers as statisticians: provable in-context learning with in-context algorithm selection. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Sys